    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.configuration_model - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.configuration_model.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.configuration_model()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Generate a random graph matching a given degree sequence (configuration\n    model).\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.configuration_model()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Generate a random graph matching a given degree sequence (configuration\n    model).\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.configuration_model()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Generate a random graph matching a given degree sequence (configuration\n  model).\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nGenerate a random graph matching a given degree sequence (configuration model).\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L20417-L20573){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.configuration_model(\n    degree_sequence,\n    directed=False,\n    seed=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Configuration model](../assets/graphs/configuration-model.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Three random graphs built to match a degree sequence, each node's target degree in a pill: 1, 1, 1, 1 gives two separate edges; 2, 2, 2, 2 gives a four-cycle; and 3, 2, 2, 2, 1 on five nodes includes a doubled edge, because the model pairs edge stubs at random and may create multi-edges. Every node ends with exactly the degree in its pill.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nCreates a new graph where each node has the specified degree. Stubs are created\nfor each node (one per degree unit) and then randomly paired. The resulting graph\nmay contain self-loops and multi-edges.\n\nBecause every stub is used exactly once, each node ends up with exactly the requested\ndegree (a self-loop counts twice toward its node's degree). Apart from that constraint\nthe wiring is completely random, which makes this the standard null model for asking\nwhether a network property is explained by its degree distribution alone:\n\n- comparing clustering, path lengths, or assortativity of an observed network against\n  random graphs with the same degrees\n- testing whether motifs or communities are more frequent than degrees alone predict\n- generating synthetic networks with a heavy-tailed or otherwise prescribed degree\n  distribution\n\nThis method doesn't use or modify the graph it is called on (other than recording the\nstep); it builds and returns an entirely new `Graph`, so it can be called on an empty\n`ec.Graph()`. Nodes are labelled `\"0\"`, `\"1\"`, ... in the order of `degree_sequence=`.\nSelf-loops and multi-edges become rare as the graph grows, but with small or very\nuneven sequences they are common; chain `simplify()` on the result to remove them, at\nthe cost of slightly lowering the affected degrees. For a null model that keeps the\ngraph simple, see `degree_preserving_rewire()`.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[degree_sequence]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [list[int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   List of non-negative integers specifying the degree of each node, one entry per node\n    (entry `i` belongs to the node labelled `str(i)`). For undirected graphs the sum must\n    be even, since every edge uses two stubs. An empty list gives an empty graph.\n\n<code>[directed]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[False]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True`, generates a directed graph where each entry is used as both the\n    out-degree and the in-degree of its node: out-stubs and in-stubs are shuffled\n    separately and paired, so the sum does not have to be even. If `False` (default),\n    generates an undirected graph. The directedness of the graph the method is called on\n    is ignored.\n\n<code>[seed]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Optional[int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Integer seed for the random number generator. The same `seed=` with the same\n    `degree_sequence=` always gives the same graph. If `None` (default), the result\n    differs on every call.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A new graph with the specified degree sequence. The original graph is unchanged.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the degree sequence contains a negative number, or if `directed=` is `False`\n    and the sequence has an odd sum.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~expected_degree_graph`) \n\n:   Build a random graph whose expected degrees match given weights.\n\n[](`~degree_preserving_rewire`) \n\n:   Shuffle the edges while keeping every degree.\n\n[](`~add_havel_hakimi_graph`) \n\n:   Build a deterministic graph with a given degree sequence.\n\n[](`~simplify`) \n\n:   Remove self-loops and repeated edges.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nEvery node gets exactly the degree asked for. Node labels are the positions in the\nsequence, so `\"0\"` has degree `3`, `\"1\"`, `\"2\"` and `\"3\"` have degree `2`, and `\"4\"` has\ndegree `1`:\n\n::: {#b3c68a6e .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph().configuration_model([3, 2, 2, 2, 1], seed=2)\n\ndict(graph.get_node_df().select(\"label\", \"degree\").rows())\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n{'0': 3, '1': 2, '2': 2, '3': 2, '4': 1}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe edges come from pairing the nodes' edge ends at random, which can produce repeated\nedges: here `\"0\"` and `\"2\"` are joined twice, so the graph is not simple. `simplify()`\nremoves the repeat, at the cost of one unit of degree for `\"0\"` and `\"2\"`:\n\n::: {#b76e39f0 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nedges = graph.get_edges_str_repr(use_labels=True)\n\nedges, graph.is_simple(), graph.simplify().count_edges()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(['1--3', '0--1', '3--4', '0--2', '0--2'], False, 4)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nWith `directed=True`, each entry becomes both the in-degree and the out-degree of its\nnode, shown here as `(in, out)` pairs:\n\n::: {#2472b05f .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ndirected = ec.Graph().configuration_model(\n    [2, 1, 1, 0], directed=True, seed=4\n)\n\n{\n    directed.get_label_from_id(n): (\n        directed.get_in_degree(n), directed.get_out_degree(n)\n    )\n    for n in sorted(directed.get_node_ids())\n}\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n{'0': (2, 2), '1': (1, 1), '2': (1, 1), '3': (0, 0)}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### A null model\n\nA configuration model with the degrees of an observed network keeps those degrees and\nrandomizes everything else, which tells you whether a pattern comes from the degrees\nalone. Four separate complete graphs of four nodes, on the left, have perfect clustering.\nThe configuration model on the right has the same `24` edges, and every node still has\ndegree `3`, but the clustering is gone, so it came from the cliques and not the degrees:\n\n::: {#de3bae61 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nobserved = ec.Graph()\nfor _ in range(4):\n    observed.add_full_graph(n=4)\n\ndegrees = observed.get_node_df()[\"degree\"].to_list()\nnull = ec.Graph().configuration_model(degrees, seed=23)\n\n(\n    (observed.count_edges(), observed.average_clustering_coefficient()),\n    (null.count_edges(), round(null.average_clustering_coefficient(), 3)),\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```\n((24, 1.0), (24, 0.0))\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {layout-ncol=\"2\"}\n\n::: {#df061509 .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nobserved.plot(\n    show_labels=False, width=320, height=320, legend=False, seed=1\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, ht